atise . . . explores the lives of three famous gender nonconformists in fin-de-siècle Paris.\" — Publishers Weekly Before the term \"transgender\" existed, there were those who experienced their gender in complex ways. Before Trans examines the lives and writings of Jane Dieulafoy (1850–1916), Rachilde (1860–1953), and Marc de Montifaud (1845–1912), three French writers whose gender expression did not conform to nineteenth-century notions of femininity. Dieulafoy fought alongside her husband in the Franco-Prussian War; later she wrote novels about girls becoming boys and enjoyed being photographed in her signature men's suits. Rachilde became famous in the 1880s for her controversial gender-bending novel Monsieur Vénus, published around the same time that she started using a calling card that read \"Rachilde, Man of Letters.\"","thumbnailUrl":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/10184081-L.jpg","readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"academic","narrativeScore":0,"accessibilityScore":0,"scholarlyScore":0.962,"traits":[{"id":"scholarly","score":0.9,"confidence":"high","evidence":["catalog text contains \"academic\"","meta-scholarly history-writing context","scholarly subject/topic combination","academic press signal: Stanford University Press / Stanford University Press"]},{"id":"memoiristic","score":0.78,"confidence":"high","evidence":["catalog text contains \"memoir\"","memoir/autobiography subject or topic"]},{"id":"academic","score":0.74,"confidence":"high","evidence":["catalog text contains \"academic\"","meta-scholarly history-writing context","academic press signal: Stanford University Press / Stanford University Press"]},{"id":"biographical","score":0.516,"confidence":"medium","evidence":["catalog text contains \"lives of\"","biography subject or topic"]},{"id":"character_driven","score":0.22,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"lives of\""]},{"id":"essayistic","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["literature/criticism subject or topic"]}],"me